1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Modern Marvels is going big and counting down.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
No series has saluted technology over as many episodes as we have.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Now, let's kiss the conventional goodbye and go in search of the bizarre as we count down the top 10 strangest Marvels we've ever featured.

4
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:27,000
This head scratching assortment of oddities proves that the inside scoop on innovation often lies outside the norm.

5
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,000
These are weird machines.

6
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:33,200
Join our coast-to-coast cavalcade of some of the oddest tech ever devised

7
00:00:33,201 --> 00:00:37,980
until the big moment when we unveil the most unusual wonder of them all.

8
00:00:37,981 --> 00:00:40,300
It may be a little too intense for younger children.

9
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:47,180
Now, America's strangest marbles on this edition of Modern Marvel's Mega Marvel's Countdown.

10
00:00:47,181 --> 00:01:01,181
Most technology, though fascinating, can be classified as conventional, applying science

11
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:09,060
and engineering in a way that matches our concepts of normal.

12
00:01:09,061 --> 00:01:13,780
But this countdown turns the spotlight on tech that turns the art of innovation upside

13
00:01:13,781 --> 00:01:15,280
down.

14
00:01:15,281 --> 00:01:18,220
dreamt up by mavericks and misfits daring enough to be different.

15
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:23,800
Each of our top 10 contenders is weird in its own unique way,

16
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:29,140
and our inherent fascination with them shows we all have a soft spot for the strange.

17
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:36,520
The strange marvel in our number 10 spot gets us off to a high-flying start.

18
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:40,080
Is it a bird? A plane? No.

19
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:44,820
It's the aerodynamic miracle that enables mortal man to soar through the skies,

20
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:46,580
The wingsuit.

21
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:53,680
A wingsuit's bizarre but highly effective assembly of webbed

22
00:01:53,681 --> 00:01:57,220
surfaces transforms a skydiver into a human version

23
00:01:57,221 --> 00:01:58,180
of a flying squirrel.

24
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:04,460
The only thing stranger than the suit itself

25
00:02:04,461 --> 00:02:08,080
is the exhilarating sensation experienced by the person using

26
00:02:08,081 --> 00:02:08,781
it.

27
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:14,120
It's unexplainable how you feel.

28
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:20,300
The feeling of true flight and your body is actually the machine flying through the air

29
00:02:20,301 --> 00:02:23,880
is a feeling that's almost too hard to explain.

30
00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:25,820
It is total freedom.

31
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:31,620
That was a good flight.

32
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:36,660
Ed Poloski of West Coast Wingsuits in Lodi, California,

33
00:02:36,661 --> 00:02:39,400
has logged more than 10,000 skydives.

34
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:43,220
He's one of the premier wingsuit flyers in the world.

35
00:02:43,221 --> 00:02:50,160
A wingsuit is a piece of equipment, like a jumpsuit, that you put on your body.

36
00:02:50,161 --> 00:02:56,280
It's designed to create a longer flight in free fall.

37
00:02:56,281 --> 00:03:02,680
It enables you to cover a lot of ground and stay in the air a lot longer than a normal

38
00:03:02,681 --> 00:03:12,520
skydive where you're just descending straight down.

39
00:03:12,521 --> 00:03:15,940
A conventional skydiving free fall from 13,000 feet

40
00:03:15,941 --> 00:03:19,300
lasts only about a minute.

41
00:03:19,301 --> 00:03:21,580
But with a wingsuit, flyers like Ed

42
00:03:21,581 --> 00:03:25,380
can experience up to four minutes of flight time.

43
00:03:25,381 --> 00:03:28,840
In the wingsuit, we're actually slowing ourselves down

44
00:03:28,841 --> 00:03:32,060
in the descent as much as possible

45
00:03:32,061 --> 00:03:37,280
and creating as much forward speed as we can.

46
00:03:37,281 --> 00:03:41,180
That forward speed, as high as 200 miles per hour,

47
00:03:41,181 --> 00:03:43,680
translates into longer horizontal distances

48
00:03:43,681 --> 00:03:45,140
that these high flyers can travel.

49
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:50,480
The wingsuit helps you cover a lot of ground

50
00:03:50,481 --> 00:03:52,220
as you're descending.

51
00:03:52,221 --> 00:03:55,880
Some people have been exceeding two to one glide ratio,

52
00:03:55,881 --> 00:03:59,060
some people three to one, and it varies on the person

53
00:03:59,061 --> 00:04:00,560
and their ability.

54
00:04:00,561 --> 00:04:03,740
A glide ratio, for example, three to one,

55
00:04:03,741 --> 00:04:06,500
would mean for every foot you descend,

56
00:04:06,501 --> 00:04:07,940
you're traveling three feet forward.

57
00:04:07,941 --> 00:04:10,500
forward.

58
00:04:10,501 --> 00:04:13,980
If a wingsuited flyer were to jump from a plane 13,000 feet

59
00:04:13,981 --> 00:04:17,620
over the Statue of Liberty, he could land nearly 6 and 1

60
00:04:17,621 --> 00:04:20,360
half miles away in the middle of Central Park.

61
00:04:25,020 --> 00:04:28,220
The typical wingsuit features three distinct webbed surfaces.

62
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:34,760
During descent, air rushes into openings in the webbing,

63
00:04:34,761 --> 00:04:36,840
aiding the aerodynamics.

64
00:04:36,841 --> 00:04:41,420
If you look at this suit here, you have air vents that come in over the top of the arms.

65
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:48,080
If you want to look inside the wing, you can see that we have separate chambers for the air to pressurize in.

66
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:53,020
It's designed to actually make you fly when you exit the plane.

67
00:04:53,300 --> 00:05:01,820
The material inflates underneath your arms, in between your legs, and creates basically an airfoil that you glide on.

68
00:05:01,821 --> 00:05:11,260
An airfoil is any surface or structure that provides an aircraft lift by creating reduced air pressure above it and increased pressure below.

69
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:16,580
We designed the wingsuit to be similar to a wing.

70
00:05:16,860 --> 00:05:20,960
Our body is the actual structural skeleton of the wingsuit.

71
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:31,100
As we descend, we are maneuvering our body in a way to turn this wingsuit through the air, similar to the way an aircraft flies.

72
00:05:31,101 --> 00:05:38,580
That's the key reason the wingsuit qualifies for its spot on our countdown of strange marvels.

73
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:43,320
No matter how good the aerodynamic design of these suits,

74
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:46,600
the odd thing is that they don't do the flying.

75
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:49,040
The pilot wearing them does.

76
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:54,660
Creating different flight paths with the wingsuit is as simple as tilting the shoulders

77
00:05:54,661 --> 00:05:56,780
or bringing an arm in.

78
00:05:56,781 --> 00:06:05,380
Arching will naturally make you fall faster, but in the wingsuit we tend to cup our body,

79
00:06:05,381 --> 00:06:10,600
creating the shape of a wing to generate more forward speed and create lift.

80
00:06:10,601 --> 00:06:14,160
And the tail works in the same effect as a bird does.

81
00:06:14,161 --> 00:06:20,440
We can twist our legs and create a pitch with the tail of the wingsuit to also turn us.

82
00:06:20,441 --> 00:06:26,440
You can do everything from forward flips to hard banks to figure eights.

83
00:06:26,441 --> 00:06:30,120
You can actually roll over on your back, close your legs,

84
00:06:30,121 --> 00:06:31,740
have your legs come over the front,

85
00:06:31,741 --> 00:06:35,740
and be heading the other way within a matter of seconds.

86
00:06:35,741 --> 00:06:40,060
In the words of Buzz Lightyear, that's falling with style.

87
00:06:40,061 --> 00:06:42,620
Such precise control is crucial when your starting point

88
00:06:42,621 --> 00:06:45,460
isn't an aircraft, but a cliff face.

89
00:06:45,461 --> 00:06:46,820
All right.

90
00:06:46,821 --> 00:06:49,640
Three, two, one, see ya.

91
00:06:56,440 --> 00:07:01,800
Base jumping with a wingsuit has developed into a different era now, so to speak,

92
00:07:01,801 --> 00:07:08,120
whereas years ago, people intended to fly the suit away from the wall as far as possible.

93
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:15,880
Today, they are flying the wingsuit next to the wall, virtually surfing the terrain of the wall.

94
00:07:16,940 --> 00:07:23,660
As they descend lower in altitude, they carve away from the wall and try and get as far away from the wall

95
00:07:23,661 --> 00:07:27,160
at that lower altitude as possible, and then open the parachute.

96
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:33,460
As our strange marvel at number 10 brings the unconventional to the skies,

97
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:37,540
our next oddity was designed to make a big splash at sea.

98
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:40,880
Weighing anchor at number 9 in our countdown,

99
00:07:41,540 --> 00:07:45,260
the bizarre aircraft carrier made of ice, the Habakkuk.

100
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:51,540
Conceived during World War II, Habakkuk was the brainchild of Jeffrey Pike,

101
00:07:51,541 --> 00:07:54,460
an eccentric science advisor for the British High Command.

102
00:07:57,260 --> 00:08:00,680
His ambitious plan called for a colossal ice carrier,

103
00:08:01,320 --> 00:08:04,680
stretching 2,000 feet long and 300 feet wide.

104
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:08,780
It would displace 2.2 million tons,

105
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:11,800
26 times that of the largest ship of the era,

106
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:13,160
Britain's Queen Elizabeth.

107
00:08:14,380 --> 00:08:16,820
The ice comprising the hull and flight deck

108
00:08:16,821 --> 00:08:18,100
would be 40 feet thick.

109
00:08:18,101 --> 00:08:23,600
It would be contained by, and insulated with, a wood composite material.

110
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:29,080
Frigid air forced through a network of cardboard tubes would keep the ice frozen.

111
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:37,880
Many people thought Jeffrey Pike was crazy, but Jeffrey Pike was no stranger to ice,

112
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:40,260
and he also knew about the strength of icebergs.

113
00:08:40,580 --> 00:08:42,620
Ice fractures easily, but icebergs don't.

114
00:08:42,621 --> 00:08:50,700
No matter how many bombs you hit it with, no matter how many torpedoes you fire into it,

115
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:52,620
you can't quite get rid of an iceberg.

116
00:08:54,080 --> 00:09:00,100
Ice dampens an explosive's energy by preventing the massive and rapid expansion of gases during detonation.

117
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:06,600
It'll melt a chunk or a region inside, but it doesn't actually blow it apart.

118
00:09:06,740 --> 00:09:10,760
It kind of goes, and sort of absorbs that energy inside.

119
00:09:10,761 --> 00:09:17,760
Pike's chemists added a common ingredient to ice to produce a hybrid as blast resistant as an iceberg.

120
00:09:17,761 --> 00:09:24,760
Pike and his team discovered that if you take 14% sawdust or wood pulp by weight, add it to water,

121
00:09:24,761 --> 00:09:27,760
you come up with a remarkably strong substance.

122
00:09:27,761 --> 00:09:33,760
14% sawdust by weight with 86% weight by water.

123
00:09:33,761 --> 00:09:34,760
by water.

124
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:44,820
When the mixture was frozen, the wood fibers and the sawdust bound the ice crystals together,

125
00:09:44,821 --> 00:09:46,000
strengthening the ice.

126
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:49,480
So here's what they came up with.

127
00:09:49,481 --> 00:09:50,740
This is pike creek.

128
00:09:50,741 --> 00:09:54,520
The name is a combination of pike's name and concrete.

129
00:09:54,521 --> 00:09:57,220
And this is a standard block ice.

130
00:09:57,221 --> 00:09:58,520
So let's see how strong they are.

131
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:05,360
Now let's try to picrete.

132
00:10:12,700 --> 00:10:16,580
Picrete not only was strong, incredibly strong,

133
00:10:16,581 --> 00:10:18,920
it also was insulated by the wood pulp

134
00:10:18,921 --> 00:10:20,520
so that it wouldn't melt.

135
00:10:20,521 --> 00:10:22,900
Regular ice, if you set it out on a hot sunny day,

136
00:10:22,901 --> 00:10:24,920
would be gone in the matter of that day.

137
00:10:24,921 --> 00:10:28,580
It would take days for the picrete to melt.

138
00:10:28,581 --> 00:10:35,920
In 1943, workers at Patricia Lake in Jasper, Canada,

139
00:10:35,921 --> 00:10:39,220
secretly built a 150th scale model of Jeffrey Pike's

140
00:10:39,221 --> 00:10:42,180
strange marvel.

141
00:10:42,181 --> 00:10:47,440
It spans 60 feet and weighed 1,100 tons.

142
00:10:47,441 --> 00:10:51,240
The model functioned successfully in tests on Lake Patricia.

143
00:10:51,241 --> 00:10:56,700
But in 1944, Allied war planners decided to pull the plug.

144
00:10:56,701 --> 00:10:59,300
The scale model was abandoned on the lake.

145
00:10:59,301 --> 00:11:03,000
It finally sank, but only after its unrefrigerated

146
00:11:03,001 --> 00:11:05,700
piecrete endured more than three hot summer months

147
00:11:05,701 --> 00:11:08,600
before melting.

148
00:11:08,601 --> 00:11:12,300
All that remains is the debris of its wooden frame.

149
00:11:12,301 --> 00:11:14,700
Although the Habakkuk never came to be,

150
00:11:14,701 --> 00:11:18,300
its epic strangeness has become the stuff of legend,

151
00:11:18,301 --> 00:11:22,400
making it an ideal choice for our countdown.

152
00:11:22,401 --> 00:11:25,400
If a warship made of ice qualifies as weird,

153
00:11:25,401 --> 00:11:28,700
How about art made from a filthy windshield?

154
00:11:28,701 --> 00:11:33,340
That's our number eight strange marvel, dirty car art.

155
00:11:33,341 --> 00:11:36,020
Scott Wade of Wimberley, Texas uses dirt

156
00:11:36,021 --> 00:11:39,380
like other artists use paint.

157
00:11:39,381 --> 00:11:41,020
And a car window is his canvas.

158
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:47,620
Sure, his work only lasts as long as the next rainstorm,

159
00:11:47,621 --> 00:11:49,020
but that's the charm of it.

160
00:11:52,260 --> 00:11:54,300
Prep is a matter of driving back and forth

161
00:11:54,301 --> 00:11:57,480
on dirt roads near his home.

162
00:11:57,481 --> 00:12:01,700
This dirt that is on this road makes this great fine dust.

163
00:12:01,701 --> 00:12:06,180
It's limestone and a little bit of clay mixed in.

164
00:12:06,181 --> 00:12:07,280
It helps it stick.

165
00:12:07,281 --> 00:12:09,980
And it's just very powdery.

166
00:12:09,981 --> 00:12:11,260
It's great to work with.

167
00:12:11,261 --> 00:12:15,120
It just adheres to the glass over the course of driving

168
00:12:15,121 --> 00:12:19,900
a week or so down the dirt road and makes a terrific canvas

169
00:12:19,901 --> 00:12:20,800
for dirty car art.

170
00:12:24,300 --> 00:12:29,660
Scott makes it a point to buy cars with a type of upright rear windows that best catch the dust.

171
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:32,657
Got my canvas all prepared.

172
00:12:32,658 --> 00:12:39,960
I've got my tools, a rubber paint shaper tool, assorted brushes, whatever might work for this particular piece.

173
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:43,380
I've got my visual reference. I'm ready to start, so let's go.

174
00:12:44,660 --> 00:12:53,320
Scott's drawings include original works and recreations of everything from dusty classic paintings by the masters to dirty dogs playing poker.

175
00:12:54,300 --> 00:13:01,480
I have a degree in commercial art and I work as a graphical user interface designer.

176
00:13:01,481 --> 00:13:05,360
This kind of was a hobby for me because I'd come home from work and you know it's kind

177
00:13:05,361 --> 00:13:08,640
of a high stress job working at a computer sitting at a desk.

178
00:13:08,641 --> 00:13:12,420
So this gave me an opportunity to kind of get outside and do some stuff.

179
00:13:12,421 --> 00:13:16,840
I just always had dirty car windows.

180
00:13:16,841 --> 00:13:20,700
Scott's weird hobby became much more than that after someone posted his work on the

181
00:13:20,701 --> 00:13:21,700
web.

182
00:13:21,701 --> 00:13:30,440
All of a sudden, boom, all these bloggers were linking to it, and it went kind of viral.

183
00:13:30,441 --> 00:13:36,080
And it just surprised me to no end because I thought to myself, why would anybody want

184
00:13:36,081 --> 00:13:39,660
to look at dirty pictures on the Internet?

185
00:13:39,661 --> 00:13:44,020
When the art and advertising worlds got the dirt on Scott's strange passion, it became

186
00:13:44,021 --> 00:13:49,220
strangely profitable, a success story that earned it the number eight spot in our countdown.

187
00:13:51,700 --> 00:13:59,180
Among the countless wonders we've covered over the years, some defy our conception of the

188
00:13:59,181 --> 00:14:00,071
conventional.

189
00:14:00,072 --> 00:14:05,900
So we're giving our weirdest marvels a salute by counting down our top 10 picks until

190
00:14:05,901 --> 00:14:10,045
we reach the oddest of them all at number one.

191
00:14:10,046 --> 00:14:13,740
Soaring in at number 10 was the wingsuit,

192
00:14:13,741 --> 00:14:21,100
Followed by number nine, the aircraft carrier made of ice, the Habakkuk.

193
00:14:21,101 --> 00:14:25,980
And number eight, the masterpieces you wash away with your windshield wipers, dirty car

194
00:14:25,981 --> 00:14:26,980
art.

195
00:14:26,981 --> 00:14:32,860
Now, let's bone up on our pick at number seven, the bizarre workshop where there's a skeleton

196
00:14:32,861 --> 00:14:40,620
in every closet and just about everywhere else, the Museum of Osteology.

197
00:14:40,621 --> 00:14:47,340
The museum, located in Oklahoma City, is owned by a company aptly named Skulls Unlimited,

198
00:14:47,341 --> 00:14:51,740
a leader in processing legally and ethically obtained bone specimens.

199
00:14:51,741 --> 00:14:57,080
Its customers range from museums and schools to the occasional hunter wanting something

200
00:14:57,081 --> 00:14:59,080
special for his trophy room.

201
00:14:59,081 --> 00:15:05,680
Here in the museum we have over 300 skeletons, from the white rhinoceros to the humpback

202
00:15:05,681 --> 00:15:09,740
whale to an anaconda that has over 200 vertebrae.

203
00:15:09,741 --> 00:15:14,500
Everything is different, everything is independent, it must be done by hand.

204
00:15:14,501 --> 00:15:17,220
Our articulators, they have to know their anatomy.

205
00:15:17,221 --> 00:15:21,320
Sometimes the skeletons come in as a carcass, sometimes the skeletons come in as a bag of

206
00:15:21,321 --> 00:15:25,040
bones, so they always have the challenge of sorting the bones and knowing exactly where

207
00:15:25,041 --> 00:15:29,160
each bone goes within the skeleton.

208
00:15:29,161 --> 00:15:33,640
Few professions are stranger than piecing together skeletons like so many jigsaw puzzles.

209
00:15:33,641 --> 00:15:38,020
What I'm doing here right now is I'm inventorying a porcupine skeleton.

210
00:15:38,021 --> 00:15:40,420
It's kind of a little bit of a puzzle here.

211
00:15:40,421 --> 00:15:41,620
Being that it has no skull with it,

212
00:15:41,621 --> 00:15:44,960
I'm led to believe that it was a roadkill specimen.

213
00:15:44,961 --> 00:15:47,620
And we're just cleaned it up and now inventorying it for sale.

214
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:52,440
From time to time, we have the opportunity

215
00:15:52,441 --> 00:15:56,040
to work on some very rare endangered species.

216
00:15:56,041 --> 00:15:58,000
Here, we have an Andean condor.

217
00:15:58,001 --> 00:15:59,540
This is only one of two we've ever

218
00:15:59,541 --> 00:16:01,920
had the opportunity to work on in 25 years.

219
00:16:01,921 --> 00:16:05,420
It's always a sad day when something so special expires

220
00:16:05,421 --> 00:16:06,940
at a zoo, but it's even a sadder day

221
00:16:06,941 --> 00:16:10,180
when something like this is disposed of and just thrown away.

222
00:16:10,181 --> 00:16:13,360
So we feel what we're doing here is a very important task.

223
00:16:13,361 --> 00:16:16,300
By preserving the specimen, it can go on to teach

224
00:16:16,301 --> 00:16:18,820
for 100 years from now.

225
00:16:18,821 --> 00:16:21,940
One of the first processing steps in this strange job

226
00:16:21,941 --> 00:16:25,120
is performed by Master Flenser Dale Dorsey.

227
00:16:25,121 --> 00:16:29,000
Flenser's job is to remove as much tissue away

228
00:16:29,001 --> 00:16:31,940
from the specimen as possible with a paring knife.

229
00:16:31,941 --> 00:16:35,420
This is a pronghorn deer that I'm working on

230
00:16:35,421 --> 00:16:38,120
that a customer sent in for a trophy.

231
00:16:38,121 --> 00:16:40,760
I've worked on a large amount of species over the years,

232
00:16:40,761 --> 00:16:45,100
everything from coyotes, bears, badgers,

233
00:16:45,101 --> 00:16:49,300
various types of birds, reptiles, endangered species.

234
00:16:50,840 --> 00:16:54,380
One thing every species has in common is a brain,

235
00:16:54,381 --> 00:16:56,480
at least until it reaches Dale.

236
00:16:56,481 --> 00:16:58,980
After I get all the flesh off,

237
00:16:58,981 --> 00:17:00,980
