244
00:17:32,360 --> 00:17:34,480
After the skulls have been dried for a few days

245
00:17:34,481 --> 00:17:37,580
on our drying racks, they go into our beetle colonies.

246
00:17:37,581 --> 00:17:39,580
This here is a bottlenose dolphin.

247
00:17:39,581 --> 00:17:42,080
It's been drying for about three or four days now.

248
00:17:42,081 --> 00:17:43,080
We'll put it in our colonies.

249
00:17:43,081 --> 00:17:45,220
It'll stay in our colonies about a week.

250
00:17:45,221 --> 00:17:49,000
The derbested beetles are a carrying, meat-eating beetle.

251
00:17:49,001 --> 00:17:50,320
They consume the tissue.

252
00:17:50,321 --> 00:17:54,160
They get into the crevices that we just can't get into by hand,

253
00:17:54,161 --> 00:17:58,940
and they will literally eat all the tissue from the bone.

254
00:17:58,941 --> 00:18:02,240
After the beetles have finished chowing down,

255
00:18:02,241 --> 00:18:05,280
The bones, like this elephant skull,

256
00:18:05,281 --> 00:18:07,700
are submerged into a hydrogen peroxide solution

257
00:18:07,701 --> 00:18:10,300
to bleach them.

258
00:18:10,301 --> 00:18:14,180
They emerge with that museum-ready whiteness.

259
00:18:14,181 --> 00:18:16,740
Now it's time for reconstruction.

260
00:18:16,741 --> 00:18:19,780
Each bone in a skeleton must first be drilled.

261
00:18:19,781 --> 00:18:21,560
Then wire is threaded through the holes

262
00:18:21,561 --> 00:18:23,920
to link the bones together.

263
00:18:23,921 --> 00:18:27,240
The larger the skeleton, the more heavy duty the hard way.

264
00:18:27,241 --> 00:18:29,940
I'm putting the finishing touches on the rear horse

265
00:18:29,941 --> 00:18:32,114
like here. This is the femur going on.

266
00:18:32,115 --> 00:18:34,860
It's pre-drilled already so we can get the metal

267
00:18:34,861 --> 00:18:38,852
rod through it, which basically means it's hollowed out.

268
00:18:38,853 --> 00:18:40,920
That's probably the most easy

269
00:18:40,921 --> 00:18:44,536
part, the bending of the rods, what's difficult.

270
00:18:44,537 --> 00:18:47,700
To hold the bones of articulated skeletons

271
00:18:47,701 --> 00:18:51,940
in place, the crew uses an assortment of wires and steel rods.

272
00:18:51,941 --> 00:18:54,060
The goal of the metal framework

273
00:18:54,061 --> 00:18:58,880
is to support and position the bones in a lifelike pose while being as inconspicuous

274
00:18:58,881 --> 00:19:00,620
as possible.

275
00:19:00,621 --> 00:19:03,260
The bigger the beast, the sturdier the metal supports

276
00:19:03,261 --> 00:19:04,380
