00:38:18,701 --> 00:38:21,000
Number two, Marvin's museum.

602
00:38:21,001 --> 00:38:22,000
Earthquake!

603
00:38:22,001 --> 00:38:32,040
And now, the weird invention that tops them all, a fearsome device said to mimic one of

604
00:38:32,041 --> 00:38:34,440
nature's mightiest forces.

605
00:38:34,441 --> 00:38:42,860
Our number one choice, Nikola Tesla's earthquake machine.

606
00:38:42,861 --> 00:38:49,660
Imagine an earthquake's awesome power harnessed as a weapon of mass destruction.

607
00:38:49,661 --> 00:38:55,260
believe that Nikola Tesla, the maverick genius and rival of Thomas Edison, may have discovered

608
00:38:55,261 --> 00:38:55,961
a way.

609
00:38:56,480 --> 00:39:00,580
The Tesla earthquake machine was a mechanical oscillator, which is basically a machine that

610
00:39:00,581 --> 00:39:01,620
produces vibrations.

611
00:39:02,480 --> 00:39:07,920
And by producing these vibrations, the machine could be made to resonate with different structures.

612
00:39:09,020 --> 00:39:13,780
Our top-ranked strange marvel is based on the principle that every substance, when

613
00:39:13,781 --> 00:39:16,120
stimulated, has a resonant frequency.

614
00:39:16,121 --> 00:39:20,800
If the frequency is matched and amplified by an outside force,

615
00:39:21,220 --> 00:39:25,840
like the oscillator machine, any material can be literally shaken to pieces.

616
00:39:27,140 --> 00:39:33,060
The destruction of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in Washington in 1940 demonstrated this principle.

617
00:39:34,020 --> 00:39:37,640
The winds whipping around the bridge hit just the right frequency,

618
00:39:38,380 --> 00:39:43,600
causing the steel bridge to sway violently in a rhythmic pattern that finally broke it apart.

619
00:39:46,120 --> 00:39:51,240
To demonstrate the power of resonance, we're going to see what happens to a wine glass

620
00:39:51,241 --> 00:39:53,480
as it vibrates to its breaking point.

621
00:39:53,481 --> 00:40:00,000
First, I'll start the glass vibrating and use a microphone connected to an oscilloscope

622
00:40:00,001 --> 00:40:02,640
to see what frequency the glass vibrates at.

623
00:40:02,641 --> 00:40:11,260
I'll then set that frequency on a signal generator, which is going to play back through an amplifier

624
00:40:11,261 --> 00:40:13,140
into a speaker.

625
00:40:13,141 --> 00:40:15,640
As the glass is going to be moving more than 400 times

626
00:40:15,641 --> 00:40:18,520
per second, we will be using a strobe light in order

627
00:40:18,521 --> 00:40:20,680
to slow down the motion.

628
00:40:20,681 --> 00:40:22,280
Now, this is going to be loud, so I have

629
00:40:22,281 --> 00:40:24,920
to wear some protection.

630
00:40:24,921 --> 00:40:27,280
As the vibrations increase in intensity,

631
00:40:27,281 --> 00:40:29,560
the glass will start to move more and more.

632
00:40:29,561 --> 00:40:32,060
And eventually, it can't take it anymore.

633
00:40:32,061 --> 00:40:34,920
So as I turn up the volume, the glass will break.

634
00:40:58,483 --> 00:41:04,380
So what we saw was like a mini earthquake, and Tesla's earthquake machine would be able

635
00:41:04,381 --> 00:41:07,340
to do this, but on a much larger scale.

636
00:41:07,341 --> 00:41:12,340
In 1898, Tesla claimed to have created an oscillator with an

637
00:41:12,341 --> 00:41:16,340
adjustable frequency that was no bigger than an alarm clock,

638
00:41:16,341 --> 00:41:19,340
and wrote of an attempt to use it on a building under

639
00:41:19,341 --> 00:41:21,340
construction.

640
00:41:21,341 --> 00:41:25,340
In a few minutes, I felt the building beginning to tremble.

641
00:41:25,341 --> 00:41:28,340
Ten minutes more, and I could have laid it in the street.

642
00:41:28,341 --> 00:41:31,340
And with the same device, I could have dropped the Brooklyn

643
00:41:31,341 --> 00:41:36,340
Bridge into the East River in less than an hour.

644
00:41:36,341 --> 00:41:40,420
Stories persist that later experiments with the device

645
00:41:40,421 --> 00:41:43,140
went alarmingly out of control.

646
00:41:43,141 --> 00:41:48,020
There's a legend that he started up his little oscillator,

647
00:41:48,021 --> 00:41:50,260
or what some call the earthquake machine,

648
00:41:50,261 --> 00:41:52,820
and he started vibrating lower Manhattan.

649
00:41:52,821 --> 00:41:55,700
And the call went out to the police department,

650
00:41:55,701 --> 00:41:57,220
and the police came running.

651
00:41:57,221 --> 00:41:58,660
They were pounding on the door.

652
00:41:58,661 --> 00:42:01,700
They broke into his lab, and they saw him with a sledgehammer

653
00:42:01,701 --> 00:42:02,660
smashing the machine.

654
00:42:02,661 --> 00:42:03,540
The switch got stuck.

655
00:42:03,541 --> 00:42:04,340
He couldn't turn it off.

656
00:42:04,341 --> 00:42:08,440
And he almost brought down some buildings and neighborhood.

657
00:42:08,441 --> 00:42:11,120
We may never fully distinguish reality from myth

658
00:42:11,121 --> 00:42:14,980
about Tesla's bizarre oscillator, but the unanswered questions

659
00:42:14,981 --> 00:42:17,660
give this marvel a mystique that no other entry

660
00:42:17,661 --> 00:42:19,120
in our countdown can match.

661
00:42:21,860 --> 00:42:25,000
It and the other odd finalists we selected

662
00:42:25,001 --> 00:42:27,300
